      "math_academy_id": "e-4101",
      "problem": "Solve for $x$ where $3^{3x-3}=9^x.$",
      "worked_solution": "To solve this equation, we need both exponential terms to have a common base.\n\nTo do this, we write $9$ as ${\\color{red}3}^2,$ and then rewrite the right-hand side using the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{3x-3} & ={9}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{3x-3} & ={({3}^{2})}^{x} \\\\ {3}^{3x-3} & ={3}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since both sides of the equation share the same base $({\\color{red}3}),$ we equate the exponents and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-3 & =2x \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$",
